---
// The section that turns "a ZX Spectrum can play against an iPhone" from an
// assertion into a specification. Every figure here is a design constraint
// lifted from PROTOCOL.md, not a rounded-off marketing number - which is why
// the page can afford to link straight to the spec underneath it.
const FACTS = [
  { figure: '64', unit: 'bytes', body: 'Every message, fixed. No length prefixes, no variable-length parsing, nothing to allocate.' },
  { figure: '<2', unit: 'KB', body: 'The protocol handling code an 8-bit machine needs. It has to fit alongside the game, not instead of it.' },
  { figure: '3', unit: 'CPUs', body: 'Z80, 6502 and 68000 assembly, from the first draft. The format is what it is because they have to parse it.' },
  { figure: '1', unit: 'spec', body: 'One document every client is checked against, with shared conformance vectors each one runs.' },
];
---

<section class="protocol">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>The bit that makes it possible</h2>
    <p class="intro">
      A 1982 machine cannot be handed a JSON payload and asked to cope. So the
      wire format was designed for the oldest thing that has to speak it, and
      everything modern speaks it too — the iPhone in the screenshots above is
      talking the same protocol a Spectrum will.
    </p>

    <div class="facts">
      {FACTS.map((f) => (
        <article class="fact">
          <p class="figure">{f.figure}<span class="unit">{f.unit}</span></p>
          <p class="body">{f.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="note">
      It is called RUBP — the Rachel Unified Binary Protocol — and it is
      published in full, because a specification nobody can read is not a
      specification. <a href="/protocol">Read the spec</a>, or see
      <a href="/ports">which machines are being written</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .protocol {
    background: var(--felt-deep);
    padding: 5rem 0;
  }

  .container {
    max-width: 1000px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.5vw, 2.75rem);
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .intro {
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .fact {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }

  .figure {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.75rem;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .unit {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-left: 0.3rem;
  }

  .body {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .note {
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
